citeturn6483view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({"ref_id":"turn6480view1","lineno":126}); Total lines: 207
L114: At each training iteration, both the client model and the frozen teacher predict labels for each sample $\mathbf{x}_{j}^{c}$ in the unlabeled batch:
L115: 
L118: where $\tilde{y}^{T}_{j}$ and $\tilde{y}^{c}_{j}$ denote the softmax outputs of the teacher and client models, respectively, and $\hat{y}^{T}_{j}$ and $\hat{y}^{c}_{j}$ are the corresponding predicted class labels.
L119: 
L120: The final pseudo-label for each pixel location $(w,h)$ is determined by an agreement-aware selection strategy. When the teacher and client predictions agree, we accept their consensus. Otherwise, we select the prediction with higher confidence:
L121:  | $$\acute{y}_{j}(w,h)=\begin{cases}\hat{y}^{T}_{j}(w,h)=\hat{y}^{c}_{j}(w,h),&\text{if }\hat{y}^{T}_{j}(w,h)=\hat{y}^{c}_{j}(w,h)\\
L122: \hat{y}^{T}_{j}(w,h),&\text{if }s^{T}>s^{c}\\
L123: \hat{y}^{c}_{j}(w,h),&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}$$  |  | (2)
L124: 
L125: where the first case explicitly captures model agreement, reinforcing reliable predictions through consensus.
L126: To weight the confidence of each pseudo-label, we define an adaptive confidence weight $\lambda(w,h)$. Let $s^{T}=\max\big(\tilde{y}^{T}_{j}(w,h)\big)$ and $s^{c}=\max\big(\tilde{y}^{c}_{j}(w,h)\big)$ denote the maximum confidence scores of the teacher and client, respectively. Then:
L127: 
L128:  | $$\lambda_{j}(w,h)=\begin{cases}1,&\hat{y}^{T}_{j}(w,h)=\hat{y}^{c}_{j}(w,h)\\
L129: s^{T},&s^{T}>s^{c}\\
L130: s^{c},&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}$$  |  | (3)
L131: This weighting scheme assigns full confidence when both models agree, and otherwise uses the maximum confidence score from the more confident model.
L132: 
L133: After generating the pseudo-labeled dataset $\mathcal{D}_{c}^{PL}=\{(\acute{y}_{j},\lambda_{j})\}_{j=1}^{N_{c}}$ with adaptive agreement masks, the local model is further trained on the unlabeled data using the generated pseudo-labels and their associated weights. The unsupervised loss for the unlabeled data is computed as a weighted cross-entropy loss:
L134:  | $$\mathcal{L}_{u}=\frac{1}{|B_{u}|}\sum_{j\in B_{u}}\sum_{w,h}\lambda_{j}(w,h)\cdot\ell_{CE}\big(\hat{y}_{j}(w,h),\acute{y}_{j}(w,h)\big)$$  |  | (4)
L135: where $B_{u}$ denotes the batch of unlabeled samples, $\ell_{CE}$ is the cross-entropy loss, and $\lambda(w,h)$ modulates the contribution of each pixel based on the prediction confidence. This phase enables the model to exploit abundant unlabeled data while prioritizing reliable pseudo-label regions. Through iterative federated updates, both local and global models improve, progressively refining pseudo-label quality over communication rounds.
L136: Algorithm cite38†1 provides a detailed step-by-step overview of this process.
